{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZTKj1sNwP7LXgSaNGFw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the three repair files and the self-check prints `OK`. I did not check novelty against the other models, and that is the gate the previous attempt failed.\n\n**The change:** the model refines the incumbent `local_representativeness`. Everyone still shares one over-alternating prototype. What varies between people is how much weight each person puts on irregularity (switching and not being periodic) versus local heads/tails balance, drawn from a population distribution. So some people judge mostly by how much a sequence switches, others mostly by its heads/tails mix. The rejected attempt did the reverse: it varied each person's prototype alternation rate. That is the same mechanism as `individual_alternation_prototype`, so it came out as a near-duplicate (RMSE 0.00063). This change is aimed at the one critique that survives FDR: people differ much more in their preference for the more-alternating sequence than the incumbent allows (observed SD 0.217 against 0.053).\n\n**Self-check result:** the model loads, has a finite log-probability, meets the data contract, finishes the 100-draw smoke fit in about 40 s, and has a finite ELPD-LOO (-1078.8). Two warnings to take seriously:\n- The smoke fit's PSIS-LOO is unreliable: 4.8% of trials have Pareto k > 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. That number is not comparable with the incumbent's -1398.4.\n- Convergence was not verified, because a single-chain fit cannot report R-hat. Admission's full fit will judge that.\n\n**Novelty risk:** to test it I would have had to fit every model in the set on this node's single CPU, so I didn't. My reasoning is that a per-person weight should shift the averaged predictions differently from a per-person prototype, so the RMSE should clear 0.002. Still, averaged over participants the two random effects could turn out similar. Si