eriodic) versus local heads/tails balance, drawn from a population distribution. So some people judge mostly by how much a sequence switches, others mostly by its heads/tails mix. The rejected attempt did the reverse: it varied each person's prototype alternation rate. That is the same mechanism as `individual_alternation_prototype`, so it came out as a near-duplicate (RMSE 0.00063). This change is aimed at the one critique that survives FDR: people differ much more in their preference for the more-alternating sequence than the incumbent allows (observed SD 0.217 against 0.053).\n\n**Self-check result:** the model loads, has a finite log-probability, meets the data contract, finishes the 100-draw smoke fit in about 40 s, and has a finite ELPD-LOO (-1078.8). Two warnings to take seriously:\n- The smoke fit's PSIS-LOO is unreliable: 4.8% of trials have Pareto k > 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. That number is not comparable with the incumbent's -1398.4.\n- Convergence was not verified, because a single-chain fit cannot report R-hat. Admission's full fit will judge that.\n\n**Novelty risk:** to test it I would have had to fit every model in the set on this node's single CPU, so I didn't. My reasoning is that a per-person weight should shift the averaged predictions differently from a per-person prototype, so the RMSE should clear 0.002. Still, averaged over participants the two random effects could turn out similar. Since a second rejection is final for this slot, this is the main risk.\n\nFiles are in `iter_0/candidate_4_repair_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt` (`individual_irregularity_weight`)\n- `candidate.py`","ttft_ms":3638,"type":"result","duration_ms":428761,"uuid":"3c7d73c7-82ff-4ebf-83f1-c592d57ce04a","ttft_stream_ms":2992,"time_to_request_ms":106,"first_content_frame_ms":2993,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
